    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0019",
      "sequence": 31,
      "meaning": "I bow to Raghubara’s name ‘Rāma,’ whose letters are seeds of fire, sun and moon. It is the life of the Veda, pervaded by Brahmā, Viṣṇu and Śiva; beyond attributes yet unequalled treasury of virtues. It is the great mantra Śiva repeats and teaches in Kāśī as the means to liberation. Gaṇeśa knows its greatness and, by the name’s power, is worshipped first. Vālmīki knew its might and became purified by repeating it in reverse. Hearing Śiva say that it equals a thousand names, Bhavānī repeated it with her beloved. Seeing that the cause of his joy was the sound belonging to Hara himself, Śiva made that ornament of his beloved into her foremost ornament. Knowing the name’s power well, Śiva turned the kālakūṭa poison’s fruit into nectar. Devotion to Raghupati is the rainy season and Tulsīdāsa the fine rice crop; the two excellent letters of Rāma’s name are the months Śrāvaṇa and Bhādrapada.",
